    {
      "key": "lesson-22",
      "name": "WebLab Adaptive",
      "absolute_position": 22,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 22,
      "properties": {
        "generate_outline": "Make a level for each of the following checkpoints. Label the level by the skill, checkpoint and tier. Decide what goes into the level based on what the student does. These should all be web lab levels and maybe some panels if needed to show info. \n\n\nSkill\nCheckpoint\nTier\nWhat the student can do\nEvidence the tutor sees\nLanguage of web dev\nRecognize\nGuided\nFind the hero section, feature cards, interactive canvas, and call-to-action on the target site\nLabeled or pointed-out blocks\nLanguage of web dev\nExplain purpose\nIndependent\nSay what each block is for\nA one-line purpose for each block\nLanguage of web dev\nAnalyze\nIndependent\nExplain why a block works well on the target site\nA claim plus a reason (\"the button stands out because...\")\nLanguage of web dev\nApply\nExtended\nUse block vocabulary in a prompt to get a more precise result\nA prompt that names blocks and their roles\nLanguage of web dev\nTransfer\nExtended\nUse the same vocabulary on a page they didn't build\nA short comparison or critique of two designs\nBuild with AI\nAsk\nGuided\nWrite a first prompt that names what they want built\nA prompt that names a section or a goal\nBuild with AI\nSpecify\nIndependent\nAdd concrete details so the prompt isn't vague\nA prompt with 2+ specific design details\nBuild with AI\nRefine\nIndependent\nFollow up on what came back, targeting one thing that's off\nA second prompt that refers to the first result\nBuild with AI\nJudge\nExtended\nCompare AI's output with what they wanted and decide whether to accept or redirect\nA stated decision with a reason\nBuild with AI\nOverride\nExtended\nDecide when editing by hand beats another prompt, and do it\nA manual change plus an explanation of why\nDebug with AI\nFind the clue\nGuided\nPoint to the part of an error message or broken page that shows what's wrong\nA highlighted keyword or element\nDebug with AI\nShare it\nGuided\nGive AI the error and get back an explanation and a fix\nThe error pasted into the tutor and a response received\nDebug with AI\nAdd context\nIndependent\nTell AI what they expected, what happened, and where\nA prompt that has all three\nDebug with AI\nVerify\nExtended\nCheck that the error is gone and nothing new broke\nA stated check (\"I reloaded and the image appears\")\nDebug with AI\nDiagnose first\nExtended\nGuess the cause before asking AI, then compare\nA written hypothesis plus a comparison with AI's answer"
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-22",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "katies-playground"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-23",
      "name": "Adaptive Checkpoint Steps",
      "absolute_position": 23,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 23,
      "properties": {
        "generate_outline": "Rules for every level\nCreate exactly 57 levels, numbered 1 to 57 as listed below. Do not add, merge or skip any.\nAudience: high school students, about 14 to 18, with no web development experience. Use plain, friendly wording.\nInfo panels: several panels per level, each with one image and one short line of text, about 15 words or fewer.\nVideos: one idea each, with the length given.\nInteractive widgets: small interactive experiences on a sample page or card, with no score. They can be practice.\nWeblab2: the student applies the skill in their own project only. The last level of every checkpoint is a Weblab2 level.\nMultiple choice: one question, four options, one best answer, with short feedback.\nFree response: one sentence.\nImage rules: AI is a system, never a creature. Never show a robot, mascot, face or assistant character. Show AI as a simple abstract node cluster or a spark, or show a student doing the work.\nShared checkpoints\n\nSteer It (objective 1: steer AI with specific prompts)\n\nSteer It, Level 1: Steer the AI (Video). A 60-second animated hook. A student types a vague order and gets a plain, generic page, then types a specific order and gets the page they pictured. The AI appears only as a node cluster that lights up and resolves into the page. It ends with the two moves: add a detail, or change one thing and say what to keep. Make it funny and fast.\nSteer It, Level 2: The prompt dial (Interactive widget). Toggles for who it is for, the feel, the parts and the content. Each toggle switched on changes a sample page from generic to specific, with a small animation and sound.\nSteer It, Level 3: Steer your first version (Weblab2). Prompt: “What would you change first? Add a detail or change one thing, and say what to keep.” Evidence: 2 or more specific details, or a follow-up that changes one thing.\n\nUse the Lingo (objective 3: web vocabulary)\n4. Use the Lingo, Level 1: Meet the page parts (Video). A 60-second animated tour. A page builds itself block by block, and each block pops in with its name and job: hero, feature cards, interactive canvas, call-to-action. Short, bright and a little silly.\n5. Use the Lingo, Level 2: Tap a block (Interactive widget). A sample page where each block wiggles and shows a name tag with its job when tapped.\n6. Use the Lingo, Level 3: Name a block in your project (Weblab2). Prompt: “Pick one part of your page and ask for a change. Name it and say its job.” Evidence: a prompt that names a block and its job.\n\nKeep or Redirect (objective 2: judge AI output)\n7. Keep or Redirect, Level 1: Match your plan (Video). A 60-second screen recording. A plan note sits beside an AI result. The narrator marks what matches and what doesn’t, then shows three choices: keep it, redirect it with a new prompt, or edit it by hand when the change is small and exact.\n8. Keep or Redirect, Level 2: Plan versus result (Interactive widget). The student taps the parts of a sample result that match a plan, then chooses keep, redirect or edit and watches the sample page respond.\n9. Keep or Redirect, Level 3: Decide on a real change (Weblab2). The student writes a one-sentence plan for a part of their page. When the AI changes something, the tutor shows the plan beside the result and asks: “Does this match? Keep it, redirect it or edit it, and say why.” Evidence: a decision with a reason that refers to the plan.\n\nGive Context (objective 4: describe a problem with context)\n10. Give Context, Level 1: Three questions (Video). A 60-second comedy sketch with a student. They type “It’s broken!” and get a vague reply shown as text beside a dimmed node cluster. They try again with a clear report and get a useful reply. It ends with three questions: what did you expect, what happened, and where?\n11. Give Context, Level 2: Build a bug report (Interactive widget). The student taps to add what was expected, what happened and where to a report about a sample broken page. After each part, a canned reply gets more useful.\n12. Give Context, Level 3: Report a problem in your project (Weblab2). Needs a real problem. Prompt: “Tell the AI what you expected, what happened and where.” Evidence: all three parts in one message.\n\nCheck the Fix (objective 5: verify a fix)\n13. Check the Fix, Level 1: Trust, then check (Info panels). Three panels. (1) A reload icon on a page: “Reload and look.” (2) A cursor clicking every button: “Click everything again.” (3) A page with a new problem circled: “Look for anything new that broke.”\n14. Check the Fix, Level 2: Before and after (Interactive widget). A slider across two versions of a sample page. It reveals a fixed bug and a new problem that appeared, and the student finds what changed.\n15. Check the Fix, Level 3: Check your fix (Weblab2). Prompt: “How did you check? Say what you tried.” Evidence: a stated check, such as “I reloaded and the image appears.”\n\nInteractive elements (all objective 3)\n\nA list and a random pick\n16. A list and a random pick, Level 1: Options in a list (Info panels). Three panels. (1) A list of five lunch places: “A list holds the options.” (2) An arrow picking one: “A random pick chooses one by chance.” (3) A wheel spinning: “The spinning is just how it looks.”\n17. A list and a random pick, Level 2: Edit the list, then pick (Interactive widget). The student adds or removes items on a sample list, presses Pick, and sees a different item each time.\n18. A list and a random pick, Level 3: Describe your list and your pick (Weblab2). Prompt: “What are your options, and how should the page choose one?” Evidence: explains that the page picks one item from a list by chance.\n\nA number and a rule\n19. A number and a rule, Level 1: Numbers and rules (Video). A 60-second video. A tiny game where a score goes up when something happens, and a rule says what happens next: if the score reaches 10, the round ends.\n20. A number and a rule, Level 2: Which one is the rule? (Multiple choice). Which sentence is a rule? A “The score is 7.” B “If the score reaches 10, the round ends.” C “The button is blue.” D “The page has a title.” Correct: B. Feedback: a rule says if this, then that.\n21. A number and a rule, Level 3: Say your rule (Free response). Prompt: “Write the rule for your page as if... then...” Accept any sensible rule that has a condition and a result.\n22. A number and a rule, Level 4: Add a number and a rule (Weblab2). Prompt: “What number will change, and what rule will use it?” The tutor shows the rule they wrote. Evidence: names the number that changes and the rule that uses it.\n\nRemember and show\n23. Remember and show, Level 1: The page remembers (Info panels). Four panels. (1) A wheel with three past picks listed: “The page can remember what happened.” (2) A click on a choice: “A click can save a pick.” (3) A list showing saved picks: “Then it can show them again.” (4) A progress bar filling: “Showing can be a bar, a count or a preview.”\n24. Remember and show, Level 2: What does the page remember? (Interactive widget). The student clicks choices on a sample page and watches a memory list and a display update together.\n25. Remember and show, Level 3: Remember and show in your project (Weblab2). Prompt: “What should your page remember, and what should show when it does?” Evidence: names what is remembered and what changes on screen.\n\nStyle and design\n\nLayout and spacing (objective 3)\n26. Layout and spacing, Level 1: Space, alignment and order (Info panels). Four panels. (1) A crowded page: “Crowded?” (2) The same page with more space: “Space gives each part room.” (3) Cards lined up under the hero: “Alignment makes it feel tidy.” (4) Parts in a clear order: “Order guides the eye.”\n27. Layout and spacing, Level 2: Which description fixes it? (Multiple choice). Three club cards feel crowded. Which prompt fixes it? A “Make it nicer.” B “Put more space between the three cards and line them up under the hero.” C “Make the cards disappear.” D “Make everything bigger.” Correct: B. Feedback: it names the blocks and says how to arrange them.\n28. Layout and spacing, Level 3: Describe a layout change (Weblab2). Prompt: “Which blocks need more room or a different order? Say how they should be arranged.” Evidence: names blocks and says how they should be arranged.\n\nStyle and consistency (objective 1)\n29. Style and consistency, Level 1: Name a style, keep it (Video). A 60-second video. A site drifts in style after three changes. The fix is to name the style in words and repeat it.\n30. Style and consistency, Level 2: Your style in three words (Free response). Prompt: “Name the style of your site in three words, like calm, clean, rounded.” The tutor saves the words and offers them back later. Accept any three words that describe the look, not the topic.\n31. Style and consistency, Level 3: Keep your style across two changes (Weblab2). Prompt: “Make your next change and say your style again.” The tutor checks a second change too. Evidence: a follow-up that restates the style while changing one thing.\n\nReadable and clear (objective 1)\n32. Readable and clear, Level 1: Size, contrast, wording (Info panels). Three panels. (1) Tiny text beside larger text: “Size: can you read it from across the table?” (2) Pale on pale beside dark on light: “Contrast: dark on light is easier.” (3) A confusing label beside a clear one: “Wording: short and plain.”\n33. Readable and clear, Level 2: Try the contrast (Interactive widget). Sliders change the text and background colors on a sample card and show a readable or hard-to-read indicator.\n34. Readable and clear, Level 3: Ask for a readability change (Weblab2). Prompt: “Which block is hardest to read? Ask for a change.” Evidence: names a block and a size, contrast or wording change.\n\nStory and message\n\nSay it in your voice (objective 1)\n35. Say it in your voice, Level 1: Tone words (Info panels). Four panels. The same headline in a playful, a calm and a bold tone, then a site with its tone words listed. Last text: “Name your tone, then repeat it.”\n36. Say it in your voice, Level 2: Your site’s tone in three words (Free response). Prompt: “Pick three words for how your site should sound.” The tutor saves them. Accept any three tone words, such as friendly, calm or funny.\n37. Say it in your voice, Level 3: Keep your tone across two changes (Weblab2). Prompt: “Make your next change and say your tone again.” Evidence: a follow-up that restates the tone while changing one thing.\n\nLead with the point (objective 3)\n38. Lead with the point, Level 1: Say it in the first line (Video). A 60-second video. A visitor decides in a few seconds, and the video compares a vague headline with one that says what the site is for.\n39. Lead with the point, Level 2: Which headline leads? (Multiple choice). Which headline tells a new student what the site is for? A “Welcome!” B “Find a club that fits you in two minutes.” C “Our school, our story.” D “Click here.” Correct: B. Feedback: it says what the site does and for whom.\n40. Lead with the point, Level 3: Rewrite your headline (Weblab2). Prompt: “What is your site for? Make the headline say it.” The student can ask the AI or edit by hand. Evidence: states the site’s purpose in the headline.\n\nMake the ask clear (objective 3)\n41. Make the ask clear, Level 1: A button that says what to do (Info panels). Three panels. (1) A button that says “Click here”: “Vague.” (2) A button that says “Find my club”: “Clear: a verb and a next step.” (3) The button under a short line: “Put it where eyes land.”\n42. Make the ask clear, Level 2: Fix the button (Interactive widget, practice). A sample page with a vague button that says “Click here.” The student types or picks a new label, and the page shows it in place with a friendly note on whether it has a clear verb and a next step. No score, and any label gets a friendly note.\n43. Make the ask clear, Level 3: Rewrite your call-to-action (Weblab2). Prompt: “What should a visitor do next? Make the button say it.” Evidence: a call-to-action with a clear verb and next step.\n\nUnder the hood\n\nRead the console (objective 4)\n44. Read the console, Level 1: What the console shows (Video). A 90-second screen recording. Open the console, find the red message, then point to the line number and the keyword.\n45. Read the console, Level 2: Where, what, which line (Info panels). Three panels. (1) A console with a red error: “Red means something went wrong.” (2) The keyword circled: “Which word does it name?” (3) The line number circled: “Which line does it point to?”\n46. Read the console, Level 3: Read your console (Weblab2). Needs a real error. Prompt: “Open the console. What does the error point to?” Evidence: says which keyword or line the error points to.\n\nFind it in the code (objective 3)\n47. Find it in the code, Level 1: Blocks have names (Info panels). Three panels. (1) The hero on the page: “Every block has a name in the code.” (2) Code with the hero line highlighted: “Find the name to find the block.” (3) A search box finding a word: “Search for the name.”\n48. Find it in the code, Level 2: Search the code (Interactive widget). The student types a block name into a search box on sample code, and matching lines highlight as they type.\n49. Find it in the code, Level 3: Find a line in your project (Weblab2). Prompt: “Pick a block and find its line in your code.” Evidence: points to the line or rule for a named block.\n\nUndo to find the cause (objective 5)\n50. Undo to find the cause, Level 1: Undo one at a time (Video). A 60-second video. Three changes were made and something broke. Undo the newest, check, and repeat until it works.\n51. Undo to find the cause, Level 2: What do you undo first? (Multiple choice). You made three changes and something broke. A “All three at once.” B “The newest change, then check.” C “The oldest change.” D “Nothing. Ask for a new page.” Correct: B. Feedback: undo one at a time and check after each, or you won’t know which one caused it.\n52. Undo to find the cause, Level 3: Guess the cause (Free response). Needs a real problem after more than one change. Prompt: “Which change do you think caused it, and why?” A good answer names one change and gives a reason. It doesn’t have to be right.\n53. Undo to find the cause, Level 4: Find the cause in your project (Weblab2). Prompt: “Undo your newest change. Did it fix it?” Repeat until it works. Evidence: names the change that caused the problem and how they found it.\n\nShow it off (the finish, not a checkpoint)\nShow it off, Level 1: Your hour, replayed (Interactive widget). A time-lapse of the student’s own site. A slider moves through its versions from the first prompt to the end. Badges pop up at the moments they were earned, such as the first named page part or the first fix they checked.\nShow it off, Level 2: Your skill summary (Interactive widget). A celebration built from the student’s own data. Bars and counters animate in: details per prompt (the first prompt beside a recent one), tries to get what they pictured, and a replay of the 1 to 5 closeness rating they tapped after each build. Badges unlock for the moves they used most: Steerer, Page Namer, Redirector, Bug Detective and Fix Checker. Badges are simple abstract shapes with a label, never characters. A try-next card points to the highlighted destination, and they can save it as a share card.\nShow it off, Level 3: Tell people about it (Free response). Prompt: “What is your site, who is it for, and what are you proudest of?” The sentences go on the share card. Accept any honest description.\nShow it off, Level 4: Show it off (Weblab2). The student publishes the site and copies the link, then shares it with the class: present it, post it, or both. The teacher chooses how. The share card goes with it."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-23",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "katies-playground"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-24",
      "name": "Adaptive Checkpoint Steps V2",
      "absolute_position": 24,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 24,
      "properties": {
        "generate_outline": "You write levels for a high school web development tutorial. Students are new to CS and AI. There are exactly 67 numbered levels below. Write one level for each number, in order, starting each with \"Level N of 67\". \n\nEach checkpoint includes a learn level with panels, a practice with either a weblab2 widget or MC or Free response. The web lab levels are part of a project students add to over the lesson. \n\nEvery level has its own unique Level name. Copy the Level name exactly. Never merge, skip, add or reorder levels, even when titles or instructions look alike.\n\nBe as brief as possible. Use plain words and short sentences. Explain any CS or AI term in a few words. Say \"section,\" not \"block.\" Do not say \"we.\" No to-do lists. Never show AI as a robot, face, mascot or character; use a node cluster, a spark, or a student at work.\n\nLevel types\nPanel: write the panel text. 15 words or fewer per panel. Do not generate any images for any panel levels\nWeb Lab widget: these are practice levels\nWeb Lab: these are project levels \nMultiple choice: write the question, 3 or 4 options (or 5 if given), the correct answer, and one line of feedback for each wrong option. The correct answer is never the longest option.\nFree response: write one prompt, one sentence.\nVideo: intro only.\n\nLEVELS\n\nLevel 1 of 67\nLevel name: Get inspired: Learn 1\nPanel | Welcome\nStudent actions: Reads a few welcome panels.\nInstructions: Three panels. What you will build, how AI helps, and that it takes about an hour.\n\nLevel 2 of 67\nLevel name: Get inspired: Learn 2\nVideo | Welcome video\nStudent actions: Watches a 30-second welcome video.\nInstructions: 30 seconds, with captions. Say you will build your own site with AI. Show a node cluster or a spark, no characters.\n\nLevel 3 of 67\nLevel name: Get inspired: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | Look at example sites\nStudent actions: Taps through example websites and sees what each one does.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at spotting what a website does here.\n\nLevel 4 of 67\nLevel name: Skill Check: Learn\nPanel | Peek at your map\nStudent actions: Looks at the skills map.\nInstructions: Two panels. The map shows the skills you will use. The Skill Check helps give you the right amount of support. No pass or fail words.\n\nLevel 5 of 67\nLevel name: Skill Check: Practice\nMultiple choice | Four scenario questions\nStudent actions: Answers four short scenario questions.\nInstructions: Use these four questions: [PASTE THE SKILL CHECK QUESTIONS HERE]. No right answers. Each option shows a different comfort level. No letters on the options.\n\nLevel 6 of 67\nLevel name: Plan your site: Practice\nFree response | Plan your site\nStudent actions: Answers four short planning pages.\nInstructions: Four pages: Start with an idea. Name the audience. Decide the purpose. Set the style. One question each, no A, B, C options. Do not mention the student's topic on the audience and purpose pages.\n\nLevel 7 of 67\nLevel name: Plan your site: Apply\nWeb Lab | Write your first prompt\nStudent actions: Writes a first prompt in their own words.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by describing the site you planned, in your own words.\n\nLevel 8 of 67\nLevel name: Fix it: Learn\nPanel | Three questions\nStudent actions: Reads how to report a problem to the AI.\nInstructions: Four panels. 1: A message that says \"It's broken!\" gets a vague answer. 2: A clear message gets a useful answer. 3: Say what you expected, what happened, and where. 4: Add clues, like the error message and the last change you made.\n\nLevel 9 of 67\nLevel name: Fix it: Practice 1\nWeb Lab widget | Build a bug report\nStudent actions: Fills in a report about a sample broken page.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at reporting a problem clearly here.\n\nLevel 10 of 67\nLevel name: Fix it: Practice 2\nMultiple choice | Before and after\nStudent actions: Picks what to do after a fix.\nInstructions: Question: The AI says it fixed your broken button. You reload the page and the button works. Which two things should you do next? A: Move on, since the button works now. B: Click through the rest of the page. C: Ask the AI to confirm that everything works. D: Look for anything that changed or broke. E: Start over from your first version. Correct: B and D. Feedback: A: A working button is a good sign, but a fix can break something else. C: The AI can be wrong about its own fix. Look for yourself. E: That throws away a fix that might be good. Check it first. Both right: A fix is done when the problem is gone and nothing new is broken.\n\nLevel 11 of 67\nLevel name: Fix it: Apply\nWeb Lab | Report it and check the fix\nStudent actions: Reports a real problem, then checks the AI's fix.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by telling the AI what you expected, what happened and where, then checking that the problem is gone and nothing else broke.\n\nLevel 12 of 67\nLevel name: Say what happens: Learn\nPanel | Say what it does\nStudent actions: Sees a vague prompt and a specific one.\nInstructions: Three panels. A vague order gets a plain wheel that does nothing. A specific order (who clicks, what happens, what shows) gets the wheel you pictured. Two moves: add a detail, or change one thing and say what to keep.\n\nLevel 13 of 67\nLevel name: Say what happens: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | The prompt dial\nStudent actions: Switches details on and off in a sample prompt and watches a decision wheel change.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at adding details to a prompt here.\n\nLevel 14 of 67\nLevel name: Say what happens: Apply\nWeb Lab | Steer your first version\nStudent actions: Writes a prompt about their own page.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by writing a prompt about what a visitor does and what happens, or by changing one thing and saying what to keep.\n\nLevel 15 of 67\nLevel name: Paint with words: Learn\nPanel | Say how it looks\nStudent actions: Sees a vague prompt and a specific one.\nInstructions: Three panels. A vague order gets a plain gray page. Naming colors, type and layout gets the look you pictured. Two moves: add a detail, or change one thing and say what to keep.\n\nLevel 16 of 67\nLevel name: Paint with words: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | The prompt dial\nStudent actions: Switches details on and off in a sample prompt and watches a bake sale site change.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at describing how a page should look here.\n\nLevel 17 of 67\nLevel name: Paint with words: Apply\nWeb Lab | Steer your first version\nStudent actions: Writes a prompt about the look of their own page.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by describing the colors, type and layout of one section of your page, or by changing one thing and saying what to keep.\n\nLevel 18 of 67\nLevel name: Find your voice: Learn\nPanel | Say how it sounds\nStudent actions: Sees a vague prompt and a specific one.\nInstructions: Three panels. The same headline sounds playful, calm or bold. Naming the tone and message gets words that sound like you. Two moves: add a detail, or change one thing and say what to keep.\n\nLevel 19 of 67\nLevel name: Find your voice: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | The prompt dial\nStudent actions: Switches details on and off in a sample prompt and watches a save-the-park page change.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at describing how a page should sound here.\n\nLevel 20 of 67\nLevel name: Find your voice: Apply\nWeb Lab | Steer your first version\nStudent actions: Writes a prompt about the words on their own page.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by describing the tone and message of one section of your page, or by changing one thing and saying what to keep.\n\nLevel 21 of 67\nLevel name: Ask for code: Learn\nPanel | Ask for a small piece\nStudent actions: Sees a vague code request and a specific one.\nInstructions: Three panels. Asking for \"some code\" gets a long block you cannot read. Asking for one small piece gets something clear. Asking the AI to explain it in plain words helps you understand it.\n\nLevel 22 of 67\nLevel name: Ask for code: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | The prompt dial\nStudent actions: Switches details on and off in a sample prompt and watches a small piece of code change.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at asking for a small piece of code here.\n\nLevel 23 of 67\nLevel name: Ask for code: Apply\nWeb Lab | Ask for code in your project\nStudent actions: Asks for a small piece of code, then asks for an explanation.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by asking the AI for one small piece of code for your page, then asking it to explain the code in plain words.\n\nLevel 24 of 67\nLevel name: Test the plan: Learn\nPanel | Match your plan\nStudent actions: Compares a plan with what the AI built.\nInstructions: Three panels. A plan note about how the wheel responds sits beside the AI's result. Mark what matches and what does not. Then choose: keep it, redirect it with a new prompt, or edit it by hand when the change is small.\n\nLevel 25 of 67\nLevel name: Test the plan: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | Plan versus result\nStudent actions: Marks which parts of a sample wheel match a plan, then keeps, redirects or edits.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at checking what the AI made against your plan here.\n\nLevel 26 of 67\nLevel name: Test the plan: Apply\nWeb Lab | Decide on a real change\nStudent actions: Writes a plan, then decides what to do with the AI's change.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by writing a one-sentence plan for part of your page, then deciding to keep, redirect or edit what the AI changes, and saying why.\n\nLevel 27 of 67\nLevel name: Match the look: Learn\nPanel | Match your plan\nStudent actions: Compares a plan with what the AI built.\nInstructions: Three panels. A plan note about how the page should look sits beside the AI's result. Mark what matches and what does not. Then choose: keep it, redirect it with a new prompt, or edit it by hand when the change is small.\n\nLevel 28 of 67\nLevel name: Match the look: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | Plan versus result\nStudent actions: Marks which parts of a sample club page match a plan, then keeps, redirects or edits.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at checking a page's look against your plan here.\n\nLevel 29 of 67\nLevel name: Match the look: Apply\nWeb Lab | Decide on a real change\nStudent actions: Writes a plan, then decides what to do with the AI's change.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by writing a one-sentence plan for how part of your page should look, then deciding to keep, redirect or edit what the AI changes, and saying why.\n\nLevel 30 of 67\nLevel name: Read it back: Learn\nPanel | Match your plan\nStudent actions: Compares a plan with what the AI wrote.\nInstructions: Three panels. A plan note about how the words should sound sits beside the AI's result. Mark what matches and what does not. Then choose: keep it, redirect it with a new prompt, or rewrite it yourself when the change is small.\n\nLevel 31 of 67\nLevel name: Read it back: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | Plan versus result\nStudent actions: Marks which parts of a sample page's words match a plan, then keeps, redirects or rewrites.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at checking words against your plan here.\n\nLevel 32 of 67\nLevel name: Read it back: Apply\nWeb Lab | Decide on a real change\nStudent actions: Writes a plan, then decides what to do with the AI's change.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by writing a one-sentence plan for the words on part of your page, then deciding to keep, redirect or rewrite what the AI changes, and saying why.\n\nLevel 33 of 67\nLevel name: Keep the code?: Learn\nPanel | Match your plan\nStudent actions: Compares a plan with what the AI's code does.\nInstructions: Three panels. A plan note about what the code should do sits beside the AI's result. Mark what matches and what does not. Then choose: keep it, redirect it with a new prompt, or edit the line yourself when the change is small.\n\nLevel 34 of 67\nLevel name: Keep the code?: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | Plan versus result\nStudent actions: Marks which parts of sample code match a plan, then keeps, redirects or edits a line.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at checking code against your plan here.\n\nLevel 35 of 67\nLevel name: Keep the code?: Apply\nWeb Lab | Decide on a real change\nStudent actions: Writes a plan, then decides what to do with the AI's code change.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by writing a one-sentence plan for what part of your code should do, then deciding to keep, redirect or edit the line the AI changes, and saying why.\n\nLevel 36 of 67\nLevel name: Name the controls: Learn\nPanel | Meet the interactive parts\nStudent actions: Learns the names of the parts of an interactive page.\nInstructions: Three panels. A wheel page has a canvas, buttons and a result area. Each part has a name. Each part has a job.\n\nLevel 37 of 67\nLevel name: Name the controls: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | Tap a section\nStudent actions: Taps the parts of a sample wheel page to see each name and job.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at naming the controls on a page here.\n\nLevel 38 of 67\nLevel name: Name the controls: Apply\nWeb Lab | Name a section in your project\nStudent actions: Asks for a change using a part's name.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by asking for a change that names one interactive part of your page and says what it is for.\n\nLevel 39 of 67\nLevel name: Name the page parts: Learn\nPanel | Meet the page parts\nStudent actions: Learns the names of the parts of a page.\nInstructions: Three panels. A page has a hero, feature cards and a call-to-action. Each part has a name. Each part has a job.\n\nLevel 40 of 67\nLevel name: Name the page parts: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | Tap a section\nStudent actions: Taps the parts of a sample club page to see each name and job.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at naming the parts of a page here.\n\nLevel 41 of 67\nLevel name: Name the page parts: Apply\nWeb Lab | Name a section in your project\nStudent actions: Asks for a change using a part's name.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by asking for a change that names one section of your page and says what it is for.\n\nLevel 42 of 67\nLevel name: Name the message parts: Learn\nPanel | Meet the words on the page\nStudent actions: Learns the names of the parts of a page that carry words.\nInstructions: Three panels. A page has a headline, card text and a call-to-action. Each part has a name. Each part has a job.\n\nLevel 43 of 67\nLevel name: Name the message parts: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | Tap a section\nStudent actions: Taps the parts of a sample save-the-park page to see each name and job.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at naming the words on a page here.\n\nLevel 44 of 67\nLevel name: Name the message parts: Apply\nWeb Lab | Name a section in your project\nStudent actions: Asks for a change using a part's name.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by asking for a change that names one part of your page's words and says what it should say.\n\nLevel 45 of 67\nLevel name: Sort the jobs: Learn\nPanel | Three jobs on every page\nStudent actions: Learns that every part of a page does one of three jobs.\nInstructions: Three panels. Structure is what is on the page. Style is how it looks. Behavior is what it does.\n\nLevel 46 of 67\nLevel name: Sort the jobs: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | Tap a section\nStudent actions: Taps the parts of a sample page and sorts each into structure, style or behavior.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at telling structure, style and behavior apart here.\n\nLevel 47 of 67\nLevel name: Sort the jobs: Apply\nWeb Lab | Name a section in your project\nStudent actions: Asks for a change and says what kind it is.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by asking for a change to one section of your page and saying whether it changes its structure, style or behavior.\n\nLevel 48 of 67\nLevel name: Make it respond: Learn\nPanel | Lists, numbers and memory\nStudent actions: Learns how a page chooses, counts and remembers.\nInstructions: Four panels. 1: A list holds the options. A random pick chooses one. 2: A number changes. A rule says if this, then that. 3: A click can save a pick. 4: The page can remember it and show it again.\n\nLevel 49 of 67\nLevel name: Make it respond: Practice 1\nWeb Lab widget | Spin, count, remember\nStudent actions: Edits a list, presses Pick, and watches a counter and a recent-picks shelf update.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at making a page choose, count and remember here.\n\nLevel 50 of 67\nLevel name: Make it respond: Practice 2\nMultiple choice | Which one is the rule?\nStudent actions: Picks the sentence that is a rule.\nInstructions: Question: Which sentence is a rule? A: The score is 7 after the third spin. B: If the score hits 10, it ends. C: The button is blue with white text. D: The page has a title at the top. Correct: B. Feedback: A: That is a number, not a rule. C: That is how the page looks. D: That is what is on the page. A rule says if this, then that.\n\nLevel 51 of 67\nLevel name: Make it respond: Apply\nWeb Lab | Make your page respond\nStudent actions: Picks one idea that fits their page and describes it to the AI.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by telling the AI what your page should choose, count or remember, and how.\n\nLevel 52 of 67\nLevel name: Make it look right: Learn\nPanel | Name a style, keep it\nStudent actions: Learns to name a style and repeat it.\nInstructions: Four panels. 1: A site drifts in style after three changes. 2: Name the style in words. 3: Repeat it each time you ask for a change. 4: Size, contrast and wording make a section easier to read.\n\nLevel 53 of 67\nLevel name: Make it look right: Practice 1\nFree response | Your style in three words\nStudent actions: Names the style of their site in three words.\nInstructions: Prompt: Name the style of your site in three words, like calm, clean, rounded.\n\nLevel 54 of 67\nLevel name: Make it look right: Practice 2\nWeb Lab widget | Try the contrast\nStudent actions: Changes text and background colors and picks a prompt that fixes hard-to-read text.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at making text easy to read here.\n\nLevel 55 of 67\nLevel name: Make it look right: Apply\nWeb Lab | Keep your style, check readability\nStudent actions: Makes two more changes, then fixes a hard-to-read section.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by making two changes and restating your style each time, then asking for a change to one section that is hard to read.\n\nLevel 56 of 67\nLevel name: Make the point: Learn\nPanel | Say it in the first line, then ask\nStudent actions: Learns what makes a clear headline and a clear button.\nInstructions: Four panels. 1: A visitor decides in a few seconds. 2: A clear headline says what the site is for. 3: A button that says \"Click here\" is vague. 4: A button that says \"Find my club\" gives a verb and a next step.\n\nLevel 57 of 67\nLevel name: Make the point: Practice 1\nWeb Lab widget | Fix the button\nStudent actions: Types a new label for a vague button and sees it on a sample page.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at writing a clear button label here.\n\nLevel 58 of 67\nLevel name: Make the point: Practice 2\nMultiple choice | Which headline leads?\nStudent actions: Picks the headline that says what the site is for.\nInstructions: Question: Which headline tells a new student what the site is for? A: Welcome to the school clubs site! B: Find a club that fits you. C: Our school, our story, our clubs. D: Click here to learn more today. Correct: B. Feedback: A: Friendly, but it does not say what the site does. C: Nice words, but no purpose. D: It does not say what the site is for.\n\nLevel 59 of 67\nLevel name: Make the point: Apply\nWeb Lab | Rewrite your headline and call-to-action\nStudent actions: Rewrites the headline and button on their own page.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by asking for a headline that says what your site is for and a button that says what to do next.\n\nLevel 60 of 67\nLevel name: Look under the hood: Learn\nPanel | Three languages\nStudent actions: Learns what HTML, CSS and JavaScript do.\nInstructions: Five panels. 1: A page is built from three languages. 2: HTML says what is on the page. 3: CSS says how it looks. 4: JavaScript says what it does. 5: Every section has a name in the code. Find the name to find the section.\n\nLevel 61 of 67\nLevel name: Look under the hood: Practice 1\nWeb Lab widget | Search the code\nStudent actions: Searches sample code for a section's name and finds its line.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at finding a section in the code here.\n\nLevel 62 of 67\nLevel name: Look under the hood: Practice 2\nMultiple choice | Which line does the work?\nStudent actions: Picks the line of JavaScript that does the work.\nInstructions: Question: A button on a sample page picks a random place to eat. Which line of JavaScript does the picking? A: The line that sets the page title text. B: The line that picks from the list. C: The line that sets the button's color. D: The line that adds a heading above it. Correct: B. Feedback: A: That changes the title, not the pick. C: That only changes color. D: That only adds a heading. Show the sample code beside the question.\n\nLevel 63 of 67\nLevel name: Look under the hood: Apply\nWeb Lab | Edit a line in your project\nStudent actions: Finds the code for a section and changes one line.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by finding the code for one section of your page, saying what it does, and changing one line of JavaScript.\n\nLevel 64 of 67\nLevel name: Show it off: Practice 1\nWeb Lab widget | Your hour, replayed\nStudent actions: Slides through the saved versions of their site and sees badges for each skill.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at looking back at what you built here.\n\nLevel 65 of 67\nLevel name: Show it off: Practice 2\nWeb Lab widget | Your skill summary\nStudent actions: Explores their skill summary and saves a share card.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at seeing what you learned here.\n\nLevel 66 of 67\nLevel name: Show it off: Practice 3\nFree response | Tell people about it\nStudent actions: Writes one or two sentences about their site.\nInstructions: Prompt: What is your site, who is it for, and what are you proudest of?\n\nLevel 67 of 67\nLevel name: Show it off: Apply\nWeb Lab | Show it off\nStudent actions: Publishes the site and shares the link.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by publishing your site and sharing the link with your class."
      },
      "seeding_key": {
        "lesson.key": "lesson-24",
        "lesson_group.key": "",
        "script.name": "katies-playground"
      }
    },
    {
      "key": "lesson-25",
      "name": "Adaptive Less Checkpoints",
      "absolute_position": 25,
      "lockable": false,
      "has_lesson_plan": true,
      "relative_position": 25,
      "properties": {
        "generate_outline": "Build a website with AI. For high school students new to CS and AI.\n\nYou write the levels for this lesson. There are exactly 42 numbered levels below. Write one level for each number, in order, starting each with \"Level N of 42\".\n\n Every level has its own unique Level name. Copy the Level name exactly. Never merge, skip, add or reorder levels, even when titles or instructions look alike.\n\nBe as brief as possible. Use plain words and short sentences. Explain any CS or AI term in a few words. Say \"section,\" not \"block.\" Do not say \"we.\" No to-do lists. Never show AI as a robot, face, mascot or character; use a node cluster, a spark, or a student at work.\n\nLevel types\nPanel: write the panel text. 15 words or fewer per panel. One simple image per panel.\nWeb Lab widget: the instruction is final. Copy it exactly. The widget opens with a short demo of the idea, then the student practices. Use Student actions to build it.\nWeb Lab: students add on to a project\nMultiple choice: write the question, 3 or 4 options (or 5 if given), the correct answer, and one line of feedback for each wrong option. The correct answer is never the longest option.\nFree response: write one prompt, one sentence. If intro lines are given, show them first.\nVideo: intro only.\n\nLEVELS\n\nLevel 1 of 42\nLevel name: Get inspired: Learn 1\nPanel | Welcome\nStudent actions: Reads a few welcome panels.\nInstructions: Three panels. What you will build, how AI helps, and that it takes about an hour.\n\nLevel 2 of 42\nLevel name: Get inspired: Learn 2\nVideo | Welcome video\nStudent actions: Watches a 30-second welcome video.\nInstructions: 30 seconds, with captions. Say you will build your own site with AI. Show a node cluster or a spark, no characters.\n\nLevel 3 of 42\nLevel name: Get inspired: Practice\nWeb Lab widget | Look at example sites\nStudent actions: Taps through example websites and sees what each one does.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at spotting what a website does here.\n\nLevel 4 of 42\nLevel name: Skill Check: Learn\nPanel | Peek at your map\nStudent actions: Looks at the skills map.\nInstructions: Two panels. The map shows the skills you will use. The Skill Check helps give you the right amount of support. No pass or fail words.\n\nLevel 5 of 42\nLevel name: Skill Check: Practice\nMultiple choice | Four scenario questions\nStudent actions: Answers four short scenario questions.\nInstructions: Use these four questions: [PASTE THE SKILL CHECK QUESTIONS HERE]. No right answers. Each option shows a different comfort level. No letters on the options.\n\nLevel 6 of 42\nLevel name: Plan your site: Practice\nFree response | Plan your site\nStudent actions: Answers four short planning pages.\nInstructions: Four pages: Start with an idea. Name the audience. Decide the purpose. Set the style. One question each, no A, B, C options. Do not mention the student's topic on the audience and purpose pages.\n\nLevel 7 of 42\nLevel name: Plan your site: Apply\nWeb Lab | Write your first prompt\nStudent actions: Writes a first prompt in their own words.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by describing the site you planned, in your own words.\n\nLevel 8 of 42\nLevel name: Fix it: Learn and practice\nWeb Lab widget | Build a bug report\nStudent actions: Sees that a good report says what you expected, what happened and where, then adds the three parts to a report about a sample broken page.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at reporting a problem clearly here.\n\nLevel 9 of 42\nLevel name: Fix it: Practice\nMultiple choice | Before and after\nStudent actions: Picks what to do after a fix.\nInstructions: Question: The AI says it fixed your broken button. You reload the page and the button works. Which two things should you do next? A: Move on, since the button works now. B: Click through the rest of the page. C: Ask the AI to confirm that everything works. D: Look for anything that changed or broke. E: Start over from your first version. Correct: B and D. Feedback: A: A working button is a good sign, but a fix can break something else. C: The AI can be wrong about its own fix. Look for yourself. E: That throws away a fix that might be good. Check it first. Both right: A fix is done when the problem is gone and nothing new is broken.\n\nLevel 10 of 42\nLevel name: Fix it: Apply\nWeb Lab | Report it and check the fix\nStudent actions: Reports a real problem, then checks the AI's fix.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by telling the AI what you expected, what happened and where, then checking that the problem is gone and nothing else broke.\n\nLevel 11 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the action: Learn and practice 1\nWeb Lab widget | The prompt dial\nStudent actions: Sees a vague prompt get a plain wheel and a specific one get the wheel they pictured, then flips switches and watches a sample decision wheel change.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at adding details to a prompt here.\n\nLevel 12 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the action: Learn and practice 2\nWeb Lab widget | Plan versus result\nStudent actions: Sees a plan note beside a result and three choices (keep, redirect, edit), then marks which parts of a sample wheel match the plan.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at checking what the AI made against your plan here.\n\nLevel 13 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the action: Learn and practice 3\nWeb Lab widget | Tap a section\nStudent actions: Sees that a wheel page has a canvas, buttons and a result area, each with a name and a job, then taps the parts of a sample wheel page.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at naming the controls on a page here.\n\nLevel 14 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the action: Apply\nWeb Lab | Try it on your page\nStudent actions: Writes a prompt about what a visitor does, naming one part of their own page, then decides what to do with the AI's change.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by writing a prompt about what a visitor does, naming one part of your page, then deciding to keep, redirect or edit what the AI changes, and saying why.\n\nLevel 15 of 42\nLevel name: Make it respond: Learn and practice\nWeb Lab widget | Spin, count, remember\nStudent actions: Sees how a list and a random pick, a number and a rule, and memory work, then edits a list, presses Pick, and watches a counter and a recent-picks shelf update.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at making a page choose, count and remember here.\n\nLevel 16 of 42\nLevel name: Make it respond: Practice\nMultiple choice | Which one is the rule?\nStudent actions: Picks the sentence that is a rule.\nInstructions: Question: Which sentence is a rule? A: The score is 7 after the third spin. B: If the score hits 10, it ends. C: The button is blue with white text. D: The page has a title at the top. Correct: B. Feedback: A: That is a number, not a rule. C: That is how the page looks. D: That is what is on the page. A rule says if this, then that.\n\nLevel 17 of 42\nLevel name: Make it respond: Apply\nWeb Lab | Make your page respond\nStudent actions: Picks one idea that fits their page and describes it to the AI.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by telling the AI what your page should choose, count or remember, and how.\n\nLevel 18 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the look: Learn and practice 1\nWeb Lab widget | The prompt dial\nStudent actions: Sees a vague prompt get a plain gray page and a specific one get the look they pictured, then flips switches and watches a sample bake sale site change.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at describing how a page should look here.\n\nLevel 19 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the look: Learn and practice 2\nWeb Lab widget | Plan versus result\nStudent actions: Sees a plan note beside a result and three choices (keep, redirect, edit), then marks which parts of a sample club page match the plan.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at checking a page's look against your plan here.\n\nLevel 20 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the look: Learn and practice 3\nWeb Lab widget | Tap a section\nStudent actions: Sees that a page has a hero, feature cards and a call-to-action, each with a name and a job, then taps the parts of a sample club page.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at naming the parts of a page here.\n\nLevel 21 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the look: Apply\nWeb Lab | Try it on your page\nStudent actions: Writes a prompt about the look of one named section of their own page, then decides what to do with the AI's change.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by writing a prompt about how one named section of your page looks, then deciding to keep, redirect or edit what the AI changes, and saying why.\n\nLevel 22 of 42\nLevel name: Make it look right: Learn and practice 1\nFree response | Your style in three words\nStudent actions: Reads a short intro about naming a style, then names the style of their site in three words.\nInstructions: Two short lines first: Name a style in words. Repeat it each time you ask for a change. Then prompt: Name the style of your site in three words, like calm, clean, rounded.\n\nLevel 23 of 42\nLevel name: Make it look right: Learn and practice 2\nWeb Lab widget | Try the contrast\nStudent actions: Sees how size, contrast and wording affect reading, then changes text and background colors and picks a prompt that fixes hard-to-read text.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at making text easy to read here.\n\nLevel 24 of 42\nLevel name: Make it look right: Apply\nWeb Lab | Keep your style, check readability\nStudent actions: Makes two more changes and fixes a hard-to-read section.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by making two changes and restating your style each time, then asking for a change to one section that is hard to read.\n\nLevel 25 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the words: Learn and practice 1\nWeb Lab widget | The prompt dial\nStudent actions: Sees a vague prompt get flat words and a specific one get the tone they pictured, then flips switches and watches a sample save-the-park page change.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at describing how a page should sound here.\n\nLevel 26 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the words: Learn and practice 2\nWeb Lab widget | Plan versus result\nStudent actions: Sees a plan note beside a result and three choices (keep, redirect, rewrite), then marks which parts of a sample page's words match the plan.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at checking words against your plan here.\n\nLevel 27 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the words: Learn and practice 3\nWeb Lab widget | Tap a section\nStudent actions: Sees that a page has a headline, card text and a call-to-action, each with a name and a job, then taps the parts of a sample save-the-park page.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at naming the words on a page here.\n\nLevel 28 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the words: Apply\nWeb Lab | Try it on your page\nStudent actions: Writes a prompt about the tone and message of one named section of their own page, then decides what to do with the AI's change.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by writing a prompt about the tone and message of one named section of your page, then deciding to keep, redirect or rewrite what the AI changes, and saying why.\n\nLevel 29 of 42\nLevel name: Make the point: Learn and practice\nWeb Lab widget | Fix the button\nStudent actions: Sees why a clear headline and a clear button matter, then types new labels for a vague button on a sample page.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at writing a clear headline and button here.\n\nLevel 30 of 42\nLevel name: Make the point: Practice\nMultiple choice | Which headline leads?\nStudent actions: Picks the headline that says what the site is for.\nInstructions: Question: Which headline tells a new student what the site is for? A: Welcome to the school clubs site! B: Find a club that fits you. C: Our school, our story, our clubs. D: Click here to learn more today. Correct: B. Feedback: A: Friendly, but it does not say what the site does. C: Nice words, but no purpose. D: It does not say what the site is for.\n\nLevel 31 of 42\nLevel name: Make the point: Apply\nWeb Lab | Rewrite your headline and call-to-action\nStudent actions: Rewrites the headline and button on their own page.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by asking for a headline that says what your site is for and a button that says what to do next.\n\nLevel 32 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the code: Learn and practice 1\nWeb Lab widget | The prompt dial\nStudent actions: Sees a vague code request get a block they cannot read and a specific one get something clear, then flips switches and watches a small piece of code change.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at asking for a small piece of code here.\n\nLevel 33 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the code: Learn and practice 2\nWeb Lab widget | Plan versus result\nStudent actions: Sees a plan note beside a result and three choices (keep, redirect, edit a line), then marks which parts of sample code match the plan.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at checking code against your plan here.\n\nLevel 34 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the code: Learn and practice 3\nWeb Lab widget | Tap a section\nStudent actions: Sees that every part of a page does one of three jobs, structure, style or behavior, then taps the parts of a sample page and sorts each.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at telling structure, style and behavior apart here.\n\nLevel 35 of 42\nLevel name: Direct the code: Apply\nWeb Lab | Try it on your page\nStudent actions: Asks the AI for a small piece of code for one named section of their own page, then decides what to do with the AI's change.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by asking the AI for a small piece of code for one named section of your page, then deciding to keep, redirect or edit the line the AI changes, and saying why.\n\nLevel 36 of 42\nLevel name: Look under the hood: Learn and practice\nWeb Lab widget | Search the code\nStudent actions: Sees what HTML, CSS and JavaScript do on a sample page, then searches sample code for a section's name and finds its line.\nInstructions: Learn and practice your skill at finding a section in the code here.\n\nLevel 37 of 42\nLevel name: Look under the hood: Practice\nMultiple choice | Which line does the work?\nStudent actions: Picks the line of JavaScript that does the work.\nInstructions: Question: A button on a sample page picks a random place to eat. Which line of JavaScript does the picking? A: The line that sets the page title text. B: The line that picks from the list. C: The line that sets the button's color. D: The line that adds a heading above it. Correct: B. Feedback: A: That changes the title, not the pick. C: That only changes color. D: That only adds a heading. Show the sample code beside the question.\n\nLevel 38 of 42\nLevel name: Look under the hood: Apply\nWeb Lab | Edit a line in your project\nStudent actions: Finds the code for a section and changes one line.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by finding the code for one section of your page, saying what it does, and changing one line of JavaScript.\n\nLevel 39 of 42\nLevel name: Show it off: Practice 1\nWeb Lab widget | Your hour, replayed\nStudent actions: Slides through the saved versions of their site and sees badges for each skill.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at looking back at what you built here.\n\nLevel 40 of 42\nLevel name: Show it off: Practice 2\nWeb Lab widget | Your skill summary\nStudent actions: Explores their skill summary and saves a share card.\nInstructions: Practice your skill at seeing what you learned here.\n\nLevel 41 of 42\nLevel name: Show it off: Practice 3\nFree response | Tell people about it\nStudent actions: Writes one or two sentences about their site.\nInstructions: Prompt: What is your site, who is it for, and what are you proudest of?\n\nLevel 42 of 42\nLevel name: Show it off: Apply\nWeb Lab | Show it off\nStudent actions: Publishes the site and shares the link.\nInstructions: Apply the skill here by publishing your site and sharing the link with your class."
      },
